I was driving along a couple days ago when it hit me.

Dragonbane should be a LANCE!

Who would use a broadsword to fight a dragon, anyway?  A lance is much
more appropriate.

Of course, it wouldn't have to be an ordinary lance.  There is a
special "dragonlance" weapon in the D&D modules and books of the same
name.  It seems to be mounted on the back of a flying dragon, with a
saddle.  Another kind of DragonLance is found in the novel "Bazil
Broketail," by Christopher Rowley.  This is basically just a heavy
lance used for attacking battle dragons.

Rowley also invented a weapon called a "dragonsword."  This is
basically a slim nine-foot long sword used by battle dragons to slice up
trolls.  It might be useful in nethack, but would be wieldable only if
polymorphed into some large creature (ogre, dragon, etc.).  It would do
huge damage but be very heavy.  Of course, if you find a Titan or a
Dragon wielding one, you're in big trouble!

Spears and javelins are already effective in NH against certain large
creatures that are "kebabable."  Yes, that is the actual word used in the
source!  This would again fit in well with using some sort of
puncturing weapon as DragonBane.  Why is it a broadsword, anyway?

